// @test: run-interpreter
// @tier: 0
// @level: L0
// @tags: vbc, e2e, interp, visibility, stdlib, name-collision, mount
// @timeout: 15000
// @expected-exit: 0
//
// A PRIVATE HOMONYM DOES NOT HIDE A PUBLIC TYPE (T1201).
//
// A94 made private stdlib declarations stop answering in user
// programs, which is right. It reads the descriptor under the type's
// SIMPLE name — and that slot is first-wins across the whole archive,
// so a private declaration can own the simple name of a public type it
// has nothing to do with. The rule then read the wrong descriptor and
// refused the public type as if it were private.
//
// `core/net/quic/stats_prometheus.vr:231` declares a private
// `type TextBuilder is { parts: List<Text> }`. The public one is
// `core/text/builder.vr:77`. A program that mounts the public one BY
// PATH got:
//
//     error<E400>: no method named `is_empty` found for type `TextBuilder`
//     error<E400>: no method named `push` found for type `TextBuilder`
//
// on a type that declares both. `VERUM_TRACE_CTOR=1` says it in one
// line — `ensure-load PRIVATE name=TextBuilder` — and the second line
// explains why the mount looked ignored: the type is never loaded, so
// there is nothing for the mount to point at.
//
// SO THE RULE IS ABOUT THE DECLARATION THE USER REACHES, NOT THE NAME.
// "A private type is not the user's to name" must not become "a private
// type's name hides a public homonym".
//
// WHY IT IS WORTH AN L0 SPEC RATHER THAN A UNIT TEST:
// `make check-type-name-collisions` is GREEN at 132 colliding simple
// names in `core/`, 68 of them method-bearing. The collision is known,
// ratcheted and permitted. What must not be permitted is a program's
// compilation depending on which homonym owns a hash-map slot — and
// that dependency is invisible in the source of both files.
//
// The negative half of the rule is asserted too: a name with no public
// declaration anywhere must still miss, which is the case A94 measured
// and its 182 private-only names. A fix that recovered ANY homonym
// would pass the first half and silently undo A94.

mount core.text.builder.{TextBuilder};

fn main() {
    // The public `TextBuilder`, reached by an explicit mount, with the
    // two methods the private homonym does NOT have.
    let mut b = TextBuilder.new();
    for word in ["the", "quick", "brown", "fox"].iter() {
        if !b.is_empty() { b.push(&" "); }
        b.push(word);
    }
    assert_eq(b.into_text(), "the quick brown fox");

    // `is_empty` on a fresh builder — the method whose absence was the
    // reported symptom, asserted in its own right so a fix that
    // registered only SOME methods cannot pass.
    let fresh = TextBuilder.new();
    assert(fresh.is_empty());

    print("private-homonym-does-not-hide-public ok");
}
